  "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(2)+{f}^{\u2032}(2)(x-2)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(2)}{2}(x-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(2)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is zero. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be zero.\n\n- ${f}^{\u2033}(2)>0,$ because the graph is concave up at $x=2.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=1+(x-2)^{2}.\n$$",
